# TODO - Journaled quiz publication

## Objective

Connect canonical create/edit execution with fresh remote read-back, strict
media admission, conflict detection, pacing, and recovery.

## Acceptance

**IT WORKS** when an authorized teacher request through the canonical
CLI/MCP flow can create and edit an admitted Blooket quiz (questions, types,
timing, and supported media), then re-read the remote quiz and confirm the
intended result. The workflow must retain recoverable progress and avoid
creating duplicate sets or questions after uncertain outcomes.

Demonstrate at least one real end-to-end publication through this product, plus
targeted tests for interruption, conflicting edits, and media admission.
Reject unsupported or unsafe operations visibly; do not label a saved local
draft or a synthetic transport fixture as published. Extra polish, theoretical
platform cases, and expanding test coverage belong to the final ongoing task.

## Implementation notes

Use the existing validated IR, write-plan lowering, persisted attempt journals,
checkpoints, receipts, locks, read-back, and reconciliation. Do not add a second
unprotected publication loop. Extend remote reads to validated question/media
state and detect concurrent online edits before changing a quiz.

Expose canonical CLI commands and MCP tools for list/read/create/edit, question
type, per-question seconds, stable-ID media selection, progress, and recovery.
Fresh verified Blooket state and the teacher's online request drive changes;
local draft JSON remains private automatic recovery state.

Resolve prepared media by stable ID and validate current recipe revision, actual
file format, and actual bytes before every upload. Upload only a prepared
rendition strictly below 2,500,000 bytes, never a larger original or stale file.
Reuse the same guard in UI, CLI, HTTP, browser, and MCP execution.

Run a small publication through this application's actual MCP/CLI
runtime, read it back after refresh, and test interruption, recovery, ambiguity,
account limits, and unsupported types. The existing private UI test is useful
evidence but does not replace this missing end-to-end flow.

## Evidence

On 2026-10-17, a red-to-green browser pairing regression showed that a
script-origin message getter could claim one localhost origin during sender
admission and another before the worker connects. The workspace listener now
snapshots exact owned `kind`, `origin` and `token` fields once, then uses the
same data for sender validation and paired connection setup. A mismatching
synthetic message is rejected without reading its proxy trap or sharing tokens.

On 2026-10-17, red-to-green Chrome restoration tests showed that an old
committed Blooket URL could incorrectly restore a paired browser session
while its tab had `pendingUrl` pointing to a foreign page. Tab restoration,
reclamation, and workspace re-connection now refuse pending navigation
rather than treating that stale URL as an owned authenticated tab. The
synthetic extension did not navigate a live teacher tab.

On 2026-10-17, a red-to-green extension startup test demonstrated that
stored connection objects could pass exact-key checks while proxy getters
redirected the localhost origin after restoration. The worker now copies
validated origin, token and tab ID into a new immutable connection record.
No live browser pairing or credentials were used by this synthetic test.

On 2026-10-17, a separate red-to-green transport test showed that a partial
HTTP 206 bridge response containing parseable JSON could dispatch a browser
job. The worker now requires an entire HTTP 200 reply without Content-Range
or a redirect before any returned command receives Chrome authority. The
synthetic partial job was rejected without a page script or result receipt.

On 2026-10-17, a red-to-green local bridge transport regression showed that
reusing a response ReadableStream buffer could corrupt a delivered browser
job before its JSON was parsed, losing the scheduled observation. The worker
now copies each bounded Uint8Array chunk immediately using native byte
lengths before awaiting another read. This preserves exact job bytes even
when the source reuses a buffer. No provider Save was retried or observed.

On 2026-10-17, synthetic worker regressions reproduced false confirmations
from browser script-result property traps: a signed-out page was reported as
`my-sets`, an invalid nonempty library completeness marker was accepted,
and failed saved-sidebar or Edit Info panel replies were interpreted as
success. The worker now snapshots exact own-data envelopes and copies the
native state before comparing two independent reads or permitting any Edit
Info interaction. All checks are local; live provider reads remain separate.

On 2026-10-17, red-to-green asynchronous media tests showed that the
canonical browser-write adapter copied admitted media IDs and metadata but
passed the source Uint8Array through unchanged. A caller retaining that
view could mutate its bytes while the browser surface was awaiting Save.
The adapter now copies the bounded native byte view before delegation;
subsequent source mutations cannot alter the in-flight upload payload.
These synthetic checks do not establish remote media acceptance.

On 2026-10-17, red-to-green transport regressions showed that a prepared
